<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Weather Report – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 10px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Weather Report</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Today's Weather Will Be Sunny with Some Rain Later.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Identify specific details in spoken announcements.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify details in spoken weather announcements (e.g., 'There will be rain tomorrow.'). Can recognize project-related spoken instructions for activities (e.g., 'Bring your materials tomorrow.'). Can follow the thread of conversations in role-plays about the weather (e.g., 'What's the weather like today?').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards with different weather conditions (sunny, cloudy, rainy, windy, stormy) and asks students, "What's the weather like in this picture?" The teacher also plays short audio clips of weather sounds (e.g., rain, wind) and asks, "What do you hear?" This activates prior knowledge about weather concepts and introduces target vocabulary like 'sunny', 'rainy', 'windy', 'stormy'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words: 'sunny', 'cloudy', 'rainy', 'windy', 'stormy', 'temperature', 'forecast'. The teacher also models the future simple for predictions, "It will be sunny," emphasizing the intonation on the last word. Students repeat chorally and individually, focusing on clear articulation and intonation. The teacher physically demonstrates actions for 'windy' (swaying arms) or 'rainy' (fingers wiggling down) to clarify meaning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses Concept Checking Questions to ensure understanding of the introduced vocabulary and grammatical structures.</p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If it's 'sunny', do we see the sun or the moon?" (Expected answer: The sun.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'It will be rainy tomorrow,' is it raining now or later?" (Expected answer: Later/Tomorrow.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If the 'temperature' is 30 degrees, is it cold or hot?" (Expected answer: Hot.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Weather Report"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher plays a short (30-45 seconds), simplified audio weather forecast, suitable for A2.1 learners. This could be a recording of the teacher's voice or a simple online weather report. The report will include sentences like: "Good morning! Here is your weather report for today. It will be sunny with some clouds in the morning. The temperature will be 28 degrees. In the afternoon, expect some rain. It will be windy in the evening. You should bring an umbrella today."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describe weather conditions: 'It will be sunny tomorrow.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> The teacher asks, "What is the audio about?" (Expected answer: The weather / a weather report).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students listen to the report again and answer True or False to statements written on the board or a worksheet (e.g., "It will be rainy in the morning." - False; "The temperature will be 28 degrees." - True; "You should bring an umbrella." - True).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students listen a third time and match simple weather icons (sun, cloud, rain drop, wind symbol) to the parts of the day mentioned (morning, afternoon, evening) on a simple chart.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific details in spoken weather announcements, identifying future predictions ('It will be sunny tomorrow.'), using prepositions of place ('The storm is over the lake.'), and understanding recommendations ('You should bring an umbrella.'). Vocabulary: weather, forecast, report, sun, rain, wind, temperature, morning, afternoon, evening, cloud, storm, sky, breeze.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listening Cloze Activity:</b> The teacher provides a short text of a weather report with specific vocabulary words (e.g., 'sunny', 'rainy', 'temperature', 'will be', 'cloudy') removed. Students listen to an audio weather report (different from the presentation stage, slightly longer) and fill in the missing words in their handout. This activity focuses on identifying specific lexical items within a spoken context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Locate:</b> The teacher plays short spoken sentences describing weather conditions or events with prepositions of place. For example: "The rain will be in the east," "The storm is over the lake," or "It will be sunny on the coast." Students are given a simple map of Panama or a local area with different regions marked. They listen and circle or mark the locations mentioned on their map according to the weather condition. This activity specifically targets listening for prepositions of place.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Listen, Choose, and Check:</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with multiple-choice questions or sentences with two options (e.g., "It will be [sunny / cloudy] tomorrow."). Students listen to a series of short weather predictions and recommendations (e.g., "It looks like rain today; you should bring an umbrella.") and circle the correct word or phrase. After completing, students work in pairs to compare their answers, listening again if necessary to resolve disagreements. This encourages peer checking and reinforces active listening.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Weather Report"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Guided Listen & Respond Role-Play in pairs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs. One student (Student A) receives a simple 'Weather Reporter' card with a short, fictional weather forecast for a local area, including predictions and a recommendation (e.g., "Hello, this is the weather report for [City Name]. Today it will be sunny in the morning, but the sky will turn cloudy in the afternoon. Expect some wind and rain by evening. The temperature will be 25 degrees. You should bring a jacket!"). The other student (Student B) acts as a 'Listener' and has a "What should I do?" card with simple prompts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student A reads their weather report aloud to Student B. Student B listens carefully, and based on the report, gives a simple response or identifies what they should do using the learned vocabulary and grammar. For instance, if Student A says, "...expect some rain by evening. You should bring a jacket!", Student B responds, "It will be rainy. I should bring a jacket." or "Okay, I will bring a jacket." They then swap roles. This activity ensures active listening and natural response production.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in listening comprehension and basic communicative responses. Collaboration in the role-play to complete the task. Using future simple for predictions, modal verbs for recommendations, and weather vocabulary (sunny, cloudy, rain, wind, temperature, sky, bring, expect).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the 'Listen & Respond Role-Play' and a short individual listening comprehension quiz.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher plays a final, short audio weather announcement (approximately 20-30 seconds, slightly different content from previous practices). Students complete a "Weather Checklist" handout. The checklist has columns for "Time of Day" (Morning, Afternoon, Evening) and rows for "Weather Condition" (Sunny, Cloudy, Rainy, Windy) and "Temperature". Students mark the correct weather condition and write the temperature for each time of day based on what they hear.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying specific weather details (sunny, rainy, windy, cloudy).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly associating weather conditions with times of day.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly identifying numerical information (temperature).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Understanding simple recommendations (e.g., 'bring an umbrella' if mentioned in the audio).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two statements before leaving:</p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I can understand when someone talks about the weather like it will be _________ tomorrow."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new weather word I learned today is _________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for me when listening to the weather reports today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the weather report was difficult to understand, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., Students actively participated in the listening tasks, the visuals were engaging, the role-play promoted interaction).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., Provide more visual support for prepositions of place, use slower audio for certain reports, practice segmenting more multisyllabic words like 'ther-mo-stat' in the warm-up to improve phonemic awareness).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The next lesson will focus on Reading Comprehension, building on the vocabulary and grammar introduced today. Students will read a short weather report text, applying their understanding of weather predictions and related vocabulary in a written format.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>